{% part: device-profile %}

<style>
  /* MTA route bullets: a circle for local service, a diamond for express.
     The feed names an express variant by suffixing X - 6X and 7X are the
     ones that run today - so the X is a shape, not part of the route name,
     and is stripped before display.

     Framework labels are pills, and it has no circle or diamond utility, so
     the two shapes are defined here. Sizes are in em so they follow whatever
     element size the label is given at each breakpoint; this block sets no
     font-size of its own. */
  .bullet {
    /* 1.7em overflowed a heading once a complex carried four routes, and
       stacked a row's text below its own bullet instead of beside it. The
       framework's type does not step down at this device size — every label
       size resolves to the same 16px — so width has to come from the glyph
       box rather than from a smaller face. */
    width: 1.35em;
    height: 1.35em;
    /* The framework's own .label padding lands later in the cascade and
       stretches the box back into a rounded rectangle once the glyph is
       two characters wide. Pinning min/max as well as width is what keeps
       SI a circle at the smaller layouts' label sizes. */
    min-width: 1.35em;
    max-width: 1.35em;
    padding: 0;
    border-radius: 50%;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Centres the glyph on the row's text baseline box. Without it a bullet
       sits optically high beside the destination it labels. */
    align-items: center;
    line-height: 1;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* The glyph, not the circle.
     `.bullet` sizes itself in `em`, so putting the reduction on the bullet
     would shrink the circle with it and break the shared centre line the
     column is read down. The span inside carries it instead: the numeral
     sits a touch smaller in a circle that has not moved. */
  .bullet > span {
    font-size: 0.92em;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .bullet--express {
    border-radius: 0;
    /* A rotated square's bounding box grows by root 2, so an unscaled
       diamond would sit 30px wide beside a 22px circle and push its row
       out of line. Scaling by 1/root 2 would hold the two in exactly the
       same space, and reads too small: the corners that carry a diamond's
       width hold no ink, so equal bounding boxes look unequal.

       0.80 puts the diamond's box at 25px against the circle's 22px — a
       deliberate 3px over its share, which is what it takes for the two to
       read as the same size. The cost is ~2px of the destination on a
       split subway column, the narrowest place a row is drawn. */
    transform: rotate(45deg) scale(0.80);
  }
  /* Counter-rotate the glyph so the number stays upright in the diamond. */
  .bullet--express > span {
    display: block;
    /* Undo the rotation, and undo enough of the outer scale that the digit
       matches the one in a circular bullet. The diamond is the thing drawn
       larger here; its number is not, and a number that grew with it would
       make the express read as a different size of route rather than as the
       same route running express. 0.80 x 1.3788 holds the glyph where it
       sits today, unchanged by the diamond shrinking around it. */
    transform: rotate(-45deg) scale(1.3788);
  }
  /* Two letters in a circle sized for one would touch the curve on both
     sides, so SI - the only two-character bullet in the system - is set
     smaller and tighter, which is what the MTA's own SI bullet does. The
     circle keeps its diameter, so it still lines up with every other
     bullet in the column. */
  .bullet--pair > span {
    /* 0.78em cleared the curve but rendered SI as a smudge in the smaller
       row labels on a 1-bit panel. 0.88em with the tracking pulled in is
       the most type that still fits, and stays legible at row size. */
    font-size: 0.88em;
    letter-spacing: -0.06em;
    /* The circle's padding is zero, so without this the tracking on the
       final letter pushes SI visibly left of centre. */
    text-indent: -0.06em;
  }
  /* The footer never reflows: the label takes what is left, the stamp
     keeps its width, and a long label clips rather than pushing the stamp
     off the panel or wrapping the bar onto a second line. */
  .footer-label {
    /* `flex: 1 1 0` so it both takes the free space and gives it back.
       Without the shrink half the pair sizes to its content and the bar
       overflows — the stamp pushed to the panel's edge and the label ran
       under it. `min-width: 0` is what lets a flex item shrink below its
       text at all. */
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: clip;
  }
  .footer-stamp {
    margin-left: auto;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
    padding-left: 0.5em;
  }
  /* The bullet strip takes what the station name leaves and clips there.

     It was a fixed count — four in `full`, three in the smaller layouts —
     set when bullets were 1.7em and a twelve-route complex wrapped onto a
     second line. Any fixed count is the wrong shape: Times Square has
     twelve routes, so a cap silently drops routes the rows below still
     show, and the heading ends up contradicting its own card. Clipping
     instead means what fits is shown and the cap cannot go stale against a
     future type size.

     `flex-shrink` lets the name win when both cannot fit, and `nowrap`
     keeps the heading one line tall. */
  /* The name is what identifies a card, so it is never crushed below a
     readable minimum. At a complex carrying twelve bullets the strip is
     what yields, and clips; the name keeps its floor. */
  /* The station name sits inside `.content` here, not beside it.
     full.liquid puts the heading row and `.content` side by side in
     `.col`, so `gap--small` separates them and the name clears its rows
     by ~2px. These layouts put the heading inside `.content`, which has
     no gap — rows space themselves with their own margins — so the only
     separation is the 26px heading's line box, measured at 11px.
     Tightening that line box closes the difference. */
  /* The TRMNL X draws the heading and the rows one bitmap strike up.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------
     `screen--fonts-trmnl` maps these classes onto bitmap faces, and a
     bitmap face has its pixels drawn for one size. The framework ships
     TRMNL12, TRMNL16 and TRMNL21, and every class under this theme sits
     on exactly one: `label--small` is TRMNL12 at 12px, `title--small`
     TRMNL16 at 16px, `title` (`title--base`) TRMNL21 at 21px.

     So text is made larger by moving to the next face, not by scaling
     the one in use. Scaling lands between strikes — a 1.25 device scale
     put 16px at 20px, which no face is drawn for — and the renderer then
     resamples a bitmap, which is what made two destinations in one card
     come out at visibly different weights and cap heights. See
     `device-profile` for why that scale is gone.

     `--title-font-size` resolves to TRMNL21's 21px: 1.31x the drawn
     glyph of `title--small`, every pixel where the face intends it.

     The heading takes the whole strike and the rows keep their 0.86 step
     under it, so the two move up together and the heading stays above
     the rows it heads by the ratio it has on an OG. Scaling the name
     alone is what inverted that hierarchy once already.

     Scoped to the device, so the OG is untouched: at 800x480 the 16px
     face is the right one and nothing there needs correcting.

     In `tn--device-overrides`, which outranks the `tn--base` layer the
     framework sets these sizes in — the opposite of the preview pin in
     `device-profile`, which must lose to a real device. */
  @layer tn--device-overrides {
    .trmnl .screen--v2 .card-name,
    .trmnl .screen--v2 .row-dest,
    .trmnl .screen--v2 .row-empty {
      font-family: var(--title-font-family);
      line-height: var(--title-line-height);
    }
    /* The rows keep the 0.86 step under the heading that `.row-dest`
       carries on every device, and the user's own `--dest-scale` still
       multiplies it. */
    .trmnl .screen--v2 .row-dest,
    .trmnl .screen--v2 .row-empty {
      font-size: calc(var(--title-font-size) * 0.86 * var(--dest-scale, 1));
    }
    .trmnl .screen--v2 .card-name {
      font-size: var(--title-font-size);
    }
    /* The gap between the station name and the bullet strip is the
       framework's `gap--small`, 7px times `--gap-scale` and
       `--content-scale` — neither of which follows the type. So a
       heading stepped from TRMNL16 to TRMNL21 keeps the spacing of the
       smaller face, and `.card-name` clips hard against the bullets: the
       name has `overflow: hidden`, so its text runs to the edge of its
       box and the 7px sits outside the clip, not inside it.

       Scaled by the same 21/16 the type moved, so the heading reads at
       the X's size the way it does at the OG's. */
    .trmnl .screen--v2 .card-heading {
      gap: calc(var(--gap-small) * 21 / 16);
    }
  }
  .card-name {
    line-height: 1.15;
    min-width: 7ch;
    /* One line, always. A name long enough to wrap — "116 St-Columbia
       University" — would push the card's rows down, so it clips instead:
       the name shows as much as fits and stops, which is the same rule
       the rows follow. The framework's own `data-clamp` truncates in JS
       and brings an ellipsis with it, which costs three characters of the
       width it is reporting is short. */
    overflow: hidden;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: clip;
  }
  /* The mark slot holds either subway bullets or one network tag, and the
     two want opposite things: a long bullet list must yield to the station
     name, where a tag is a single word that is useless once clipped.

     So the caps below are applied by a modifier the template sets, not by
     `:has(.type-tag)`. The selector would read better, but this markup has
     to render on an e-ink device whose browser version is not ours to
     choose, and an unsupported selector drops the whole rule silently —
     which here would mean shipping the unreadable tags this fixes. */
  .bullet-strip--tag {
    max-width: none;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .bullet-strip {
    display: flex;
    /* The marks never give way. A station name is clipped and still
       readable — "116 St-Columbia Uni…" tells a rider which station —
       where a clipped bullet row loses whole routes, and a route either
       stops here or it does not. The name shrinks first. */
    flex-shrink: 0;
    /* A ceiling as well as shrink, so a long route list cannot claim the
       heading. Half is enough for six marks, which is as many as tell a
       rider anything the name has not.

       A ceiling matters even though the strip shrinks first: without it
       a twelve-route complex would push the station name out of its own
       heading entirely, and a card with no name is worse than one with
       no bullets. */
    max-width: 50%;
    /* Not `inherit`: that takes the heading row's `gap--small` (7px), which
       is the right distance between the station name and the strip but too
       much between the bullets themselves — a route list reads as one
       object, and 7px at a 1.35em bullet spaces it out into separate marks.
       4px groups them while keeping the circles clear of each other. */
    gap: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
  }
  /* A heading tag names the network — LIRR, Metro North, Bus, Ferry — and
     shares its slot with the subway's route bullets. Unlike a bullet it is
     words, so it needs bounding: without this a long one wraps onto a second
     line, pushes past the card's edge into the heading beside it, and
     crushes the station name it was meant to sit next to.

     The station name is what a rider reads first, so the tag is the one that
     gives way: it will not grow past a third of the heading, will not wrap,
     and ellipsizes when it must. */
  .type-tag {
    /* Smaller than the heading it sits beside, so a four-bullet complex and
       a long network name both clear the card edge. */
    font-size: 0.82em;
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    /* A floor in `ch`, as on `.row-badge`, and for the same reason: the tag
       shares the heading's mark slot with the subway's bullets, and that
       slot is sized for circles. Without a floor "LIRR", "Bus" and "Ferry"
       were squeezed to an unreadable black square — the strip shrank them
       as if they were bullets, which is what they are standing in for but
       not what they are shaped like.

       The width then follows the content rather than a percentage. 33% of
       the heading is 11ch on a full card and far less on a split one, so a
       fixed share clipped "Metro North" from both ends — it rendered as
       "tro No" and ran under the station name. `max-content` lets the tag
       be as wide as its word and no wider; `.card-name`'s own floor is
       what stops it taking the whole heading. */
    min-width: 5ch;
    width: max-content;
    max-width: 100%;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    /* Match the bullets beside it, so the heading marks share a baseline
       box whichever kind a card carries. */
    height: 1.35em;
    /* As on `.row-badge`: the framework's 5px either side is more air than
       a network name needs, and the heading gives the width to the station
       name instead. */
    padding-left: 3px;
    padding-right: 3px;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: clip;
    white-space: nowrap;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
  /* Rows are one line each, and the space that buys is given back as
     breathing room between them — the panel is read at a glance from
     across a room, and rows packed tight are what make it hard to track
     one across its columns.

     As margin, not padding. Padding sits *inside* the box, so once a row
     can be inverted the gap inverts with it and two urgent rows meet in a
     continuous black band — the highlight stops reading as one row. Margin
     keeps the gap outside the highlight, so an inverted row is a discrete
     block with white either side of it whatever its neighbours are. */
  .row-line {
    margin-top: 0.14em;
    margin-bottom: 0.14em;
    padding-top: 0.1em;
    padding-bottom: 0.1em;
    /* The highlight's inset, carried by every row so that becoming urgent
       changes a row's colour and nothing else. On an ordinary row it is
       invisible; on an urgent one it keeps the glyphs off the black edge. */
    padding-left: 0.3em;
    padding-right: 0.3em;
  }
  /* The clock is fixed width and never gives way. Without this the flex row
     squeezes it like any other text child, and a long destination pushes
     the digits into the badge beside them — the destination is what should
     yield, since a half-read station name is still useful and a half-read
     departure time is not. */
  .row-time {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    /* Separates the digits from the destination clipping into them. */
    /* Negative, against the row's own 7px `gap--small`. The gap is right
       between the badge and the destination, where two blocks of text
       need separating, but wasteful before a right-aligned clock that
       already has its own whitespace built in. Pulling 3px back goes to
       the destination, which is the only element short of room. */
    margin-left: -3px;
    /* The size comes from the framework class on the element
       (`value--xsmall`, 20px), not from here. An `em` rule was tried and
       is a no-op: `font-size: 1em` computes against the inherited size,
       so the class always won and the clock stayed the smaller of the two.

       The clock is the row's primary fact — a rider scans times first — so
       it takes the larger step, against the destination's 16px. The next
       step up (`title--base`, 26px) was tried and is too much: it crushed
       a split subway destination to three characters and pushed the last
       row of each card out of its box. */
    /* The board's face is a bitmap with a real bold cut, so this picks a
       drawn weight rather than synthesising one — which on a 1-bit panel
       is the difference between heavier and blurred. */
    font-weight: bold;
    /* Pulls the digits together without touching their size. Tabular
       figures are spaced for columns of numbers, which is wider than a
       clock needs. */
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  /* The departure text was thin enough to read as grey from across the
     room. The face has a bold cut, so this picks a drawn weight rather
     than synthesising one.

     The destination is deliberately left at the normal weight. Bold glyphs
     are wider, and the destination is the one element on the row with no
     width to spare — bolding it cost three characters in a split subway
     column, where "Coney Island-Stillwell Av" was already down to eight.
     It is also the row's least urgent element: the badge says which train
     and the clock says when, both of which now carry the weight. */
  .row-badge,
  .card-name,
  .type-tag {
    font-weight: bold;
  }
  /* A subway card splits into two direction columns, so its rows get half
     the width every other card's do — 122px against 253px. Badge, clock and
     gaps take 78px of that, leaving 44px for the destination: six
     characters, which renders "Coney Island-Stillwell Av" as "Cone…".

     Shrinking the clock on split cards would buy that width back, and is
     the wrong trade: a panel read from across a room has one element that
     must never shrink, and it is the time. Two columns of clocks at
     different sizes also read as a mistake rather than as a hierarchy.

     So every time on the board is the same size, and the destination is
     what gives way — it clips, which a station name survives, where a
     half-size clock is simply harder to read. */
  /* An urgent row is inverted whole, which is the strongest mark a 1-bit
     panel has and the only one that reads at row scale.

     `filter: invert(1)` flips what is painted, and nothing is painted
     behind a transparent row — so the row needs an explicit white ground
     to invert *into* black. Without it the text flips to white and
     disappears against the page.

     It adds no geometry of its own. The inset was once a negative margin
     plus matching padding, so the highlight could reach the card edge
     without narrowing the row — but a negative margin pulls the row
     outside its parent, and the parent clips, so the left edge of every
     highlight was shaved off. Worse, only urgent rows moved, so a
     highlight visibly shifted its own row relative to its neighbours.

     Every row carries the inset instead, urgent or not: see `.row-line`.
     An urgent row then differs from an ordinary one in exactly one way,
     which is the colour. */
  /* A clamped row must not leave a black bar behind.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------
     The Content Limiter does not hide the row that straddles its
     budget — it "clamp[s] the partially fitting direct child to the
     remaining available space", setting a max-height and cutting the
     text. On an ordinary row that is invisible. On an urgent one the
     box keeps `background: #fff` under `invert`, so what remains is a
     solid black band with no text in it: measured 23px tall on the
     side-by-side mashup.

     `overflow: hidden` on the row confines the clamp, and collapsing
     the padding of a clamped row keeps it from reserving height it can
     no longer fill. The limiter marks the row it clamped with an
     inline `max-height`, which is what this keys on, so an ordinary
     unclamped urgent row is untouched. */
  .row-line[style*="max-height"] {
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .row-line--urgent[style*="max-height"] {
    background: transparent;
    filter: none;
  }
  .row-line--urgent {
    background: #fff;
  }

  /* Grid and flex children floor at their content's width
     (`min-width: auto`), so a long destination pushes a column past its
     track and `.layout`'s `overflow: hidden` clips it against the card's
     border. `min-width: 0` lets the track win. */
  {% part: cell-geometry %}
  /* Nothing inside may be wider than the track it sits in. A grid or
     flex child floors at `min-width: auto` — its content's width — so a
     long destination or a wide badge pushes the column past its track
     and, with `.layout`'s `overflow: hidden`, straight under the card's
     right border. That is the clipped departure time. `min-width: 0`
     lets the track win; `max-width: 100%` stops anything inside the row
     doing the same thing one level down. */
  .trmnl .layout .col,
  .trmnl .layout .content,
  .trmnl .layout .row-line {
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 100%;
  }


  /* The empty state is a line of its own, not a row.
     
     It was a `<span>` carrying `.row-dest` and `w--full`, which centred
     nothing: width does not apply to an inline element, so the span was
     only as wide as its text and `text-align` had no room to work in.
     `.row-dest`'s clipping rules are for a flex row and did not belong
     here either. A block element sized by its parent is all it needs.
     
     Sized to match a destination, since that is what it stands in for. */
  .row-empty {
    font-size: calc(0.86em * var(--dest-scale, 1));
    /* Stand off the card heading by the same distance a direction
       heading does. Without it the line sits flush against the rule and
       reads as part of the title rather than as the card's content —
       every other card has a heading in that space. */
    margin-top: 0.35em;
  }
  /* The destination takes whatever the badge and the clock leave, and clips
     rather than wrapping: a wrapped name would make its row taller than its
     neighbours, and on a fixed panel a name cut mid-word still reads. */
  .row-dest {
    /* Every element on a row draws in the same face.
       `screen--fonts-trmnl` maps the framework's classes onto bitmap
       faces, and they are not interchangeable: `.description` is TRMNL12
       where `.label` and `.title--small` are TRMNL16. Both compute to
       16px, so the difference does not show in the CSS — it shows on the
       panel, as a bus or ferry destination drawn visibly larger than the
       badge beside it. The destination carries `.title--small` now, like
       the rest of the row.

       `--dest-scale` is set inline from the board's `ds`, so the user's
       choice reaches a stylesheet that cannot see custom fields. Defaults
       to 1 when the board omits it, which it does at the default size. */
    /* `title--small` (16px) against the clock's `value--xsmall` (20px);
       both are the same bitmap face, so only the size differs. The
       destination is supporting detail — the badge says which train and
       the clock says when — and it is the only element whose useful
       length varies by station, so the size it gives up buys more of the
       name.

       The user's scale multiplies whatever the class sets. */
    /* A step under the row. The framework has no class below
       `title--small` — every size variable bottoms out at 16px — so the
       step is relative, which also keeps it multiplying by the user's
       own `--dest-scale`. */
    font-size: calc(0.86em * var(--dest-scale, 1));
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: clip;
  }
  /* A row badge is a route name — a railroad branch, a bus route — and some
     are long: "City Terminal Zone" is LIRR's name for Penn/Atlantic/Grand
     Central service. Unbounded it wraps to a second line and makes that one
     row taller than its neighbours, which is what uniform row height exists
     to prevent: a column you can scan down.

     So a badge never wraps. It keeps its own width where it fits and
     ellipsizes where it does not, and the destination beside it gives way
     first — the route is what a rider scans for. */
  .row-badge {
    /* Each badge is as wide as its own route name and no wider.
       `min-width` is deliberately absent: a floor of 4.5ch padded every
       short name out to the same box, so "M42" and "SIM8" were
       indistinguishable rectangles and a bus column read as one repeated
       shape. The floor existed to stop "M42" being truncated to "M…" —
       which nothing does any more, since the row clips rather than
       ellipsizing and a clipped "M4" still reads as the M42. */
    width: max-content;
    /* `max-content` sets the width; this stops the flex row taking it back.
       Without it a narrow bus column squeezed "M42" and "SIM8" to a single
       letter — the share below is a *ceiling* for long railroad branches,
       not a target, and flex was treating it as the latter. A route name
       is the one thing on the row that must survive intact: "M4" still
       reads as the M42, but "M" reads as a different route. */
    /* The clock must never be pushed off the row. With `flex-shrink: 0`
       here and on `.row-time`, the destination was the only element that
       could give way; once it hit zero the badge kept its full width and
       shoved the time past the card's right edge, where `overflow:
       hidden` cut it off. A railroad badge is the wide case —
       "Hempstead", "Ronkonkoma" — which is why the railroad card clipped
       while the subway one did not. The badge now yields and clips its
       own text, which it is already set up to do. */
    /* The badge never shrinks. A route name is the row's identity —
       "Q103" truncated to "Q10" names a bus that does not exist, while a
       destination cut mid-word still reads. Letting the badge give way
       would protect the clock at the identity's expense; the destination
       yields first instead, which is what `.row-dest`'s `min-width: 0`
       is for. */
    flex-shrink: 0;
    /* A third of the row. A character count fitted every branch but "City
       Terminal Zone" and took a fixed slice of a split column, where the
       destination has least to spare; a share scales with the space the
       card actually has. */
    max-width: 50%;
    /* A railroad, bus or ferry badge sits in the same column as a subway
       bullet and must read as the same kind of mark, at the same size.
       The framework's `.label` padding made it visibly taller, so the box
       is pinned to the bullet's own 1.35em.

       The type is *not* stepped down. It was at 0.86em, which made a bus
       or ferry row's text visibly smaller than a train's — the rows then
       looked like different kinds of information rather than the same
       information from different networks. */
    height: 1.35em;
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    /* `.label--filled` pads 5px either side, which is tuned for a label
       standing alone. In a row it is 10px taken from the destination, the
       one element with none to spare — and a route name needs only enough
       air to stay off the fill's edge. */
    padding-left: 3px;
    padding-right: 3px;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: clip;
    white-space: nowrap;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  /* The direction marker: one box, clipped to an arrow.

     Earlier shapes hung borders and a pseudo-element off a zero-size box.
     That puts ink outside the element, and `.row-dest` sets
     `overflow: hidden`, so the parts that stuck out were clipped away —
     on the panel the shaft vanished entirely while Chrome still drew it.

     `clip-path` keeps every pixel inside the box, so there is nothing to
     clip. The polygon is a shaft across the vertical middle and a head
     filling the right third; the whole thing is 10x8, and every vertex
     lands on a whole pixel because the panel has no grey to dither into. */
  .row-arrow {
    display: inline-block;
    vertical-align: middle;
    position: relative;
    top: -1px;
    width: 10px;
    height: 8px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    background: currentColor;
    margin-right: 3px;
  }
  /* Point left: head on the left third, shaft running right. */
  .row-arrow--in {
    clip-path: polygon(0 50%, 4px 0, 4px 2px, 10px 2px, 10px 6px, 4px 6px, 4px 100%);
  }
  /* Point right: the same shape mirrored. */
  .row-arrow--out {
    clip-path: polygon(10px 50%, 6px 0, 6px 2px, 0 2px, 0 6px, 6px 6px, 6px 100%);
  }
</style>

{% comment %}
  A quarter of the panel, narrow and short — 400x240 on a TRMNL OG.

  Three columns would leave about 130px each, well under the width at which a
  destination stops reading: the full board already clips "STEINWAY via
  STEINWAY ST" at 257px. So this shows **one column of cards stacked**, taking
  the slots in order and letting the Content Limiter stop wherever the height
  runs out.

  At a quarter panel the budget is tight enough that this usually shows one
  card and part of a second. That is the intended reading: a quadrant is a
  glance at the next few departures, not a board.

  Each card is capped to a few rows rather than letting the limiter spend the
  whole budget on the first one. Without the cap the top card's sixteen
  departures fill the column and the rest never appears.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  32px for the title bar, plus the 20px of vertical padding the framework
  puts on `.layout` inside a mashup cell (`padding: var(--gap)`, top and
  bottom) — which the earlier budgets never counted, so the rows ran past
  what the cell actually draws and pushed the footer into the clipped
  strip.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  Not a `.view` — the renderer already wraps this markup in a
  `view` + `view--quadrant` div, and a second `.view` nested inside it
  drops the footer off the bottom of the panel.

  `.view` is a column flex whose height comes from `.view--quadrant`
  (`height: var(--quadrant-h)`). Only the outer one carries that class, so an
  inner one grows to fit its content, `.layout` expands with it, and the
  `.title_bar` after it is pushed below the screen.

  A plain wrapper is all that is needed: it exists only to carry
  `--dest-scale`, and the layout and title bar beneath it are already the
  two children the framework expects.
{% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
  The row budget, and why it is explicit.

  `full.liquid` has always rendered correctly and this is how: it computes
  a pixel budget and passes it as `data-content-max-height`. Removing that
  attribute from these layouts (5660c6d) made them stop trimming entirely
  — 85 rows reached one card — because the framework's limiter cannot run
  here. Its chain is

    load -> scheduleTerminalize() -> rAF -> rAF -> applyContentLimits()

  and TRMNL's renderer "freeze[s] setTimeout, setInterval +
  requestAnimationFrame before the screenshot", with code scheduled after
  initial load never running. Two rAF ticks past load is exactly that.
  The same freeze is why a probe script printed nothing on the panel.

  So the budget has to be computed here, from the frame's own geometry:

    .mashup      = screen - gap*2                  (460 tall)
    rows 1fr 1fr, gap 10  => half cell = (460-10)/2 = 225
    .title_bar in a mashup = --title-bar-small-height = 32
    .layout padding = var(--gap) = 10, top and bottom

  Expressed as a share of the device's height, the way full.liquid does
  it — that layout is the one proven on hardware, and a proportion
  follows a 400px panel and a 2560px one alike, where subtracting a
  fixed gap and title-bar height would not. Floored to whole rows so
  the cut lands between rows rather than through one.
{% endcomment %}
{% part: panel-height %}
{% comment %}
  One budget, because a quadrant shows one card.

  A quadrant is a quarter of the screen — half the width and half the
  height of the full board — so it holds one card. half_vertical is a
  full-height column and stacks two, which is why it needs a split budget
  and `grow basis--0`; here the single card simply takes the cell, and
  `split_pct` of the device height is what that cell is.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign full_rows = panel_height | times: split_pct | divided_by: 100 | divided_by: row_pitch %}
{% assign full_height = full_rows | times: row_pitch %}
<div class="layout"{% if ds %} style="--dest-scale: {{ ds }}"{% endif %}>
{% comment %}
  Every card switched off, which `boardFromJson` sends as an empty `c`.

  A blank cell is not an answer: an e-ink panel holds its last image
  through a failure, so a mashup cell that renders nothing reads as a
  crash rather than as a choice.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign card_total = c | size %}
{% if card_total == 0 %}
  <div class="flex flex--center h--full">
    <span class="row-empty title title--small text--center">No stations set · add one in settings</span>
  </div>
</div>
{% else %}
            <div class="col gap--small h--full col--start">
{% comment %}
  One card.
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  A quadrant is 385x225. The framework scales no type down for it —
  `--ui-scale` is `--device-ui-scale * --modifier-scale` and no view or
  mashup class sets either, and there are no `@container` rules — so the
  full board's 16px and 20px faces are drawn in a quarter of the area.

  One station, read at a glance, is what that space is actually for.

  **The first card present, not slot 1.** A slot number is a position in
  the full-screen grid, not a ranking, and a board whose slot 1 is off is
  an ordinary board — so requiring that slot would draw a blank panel for
  someone who had configured five cards. The backend omits a hidden card
  from the payload and leaves the others on their own slots, so "first
  present" is simply the first card this loop meets.
{% endcomment %}
      {% assign drawn = 0 %}
      {% for card in c %}
        {% if drawn < 1 %}
          {% assign drawn = drawn | plus: 1 %}
          {% assign stack_height = full_height %}
        <div class="w--full">

        <div class="card-heading flex flex--row flex--center-y gap--small">
          <span class="card-name title title--small md:title--base lg:title--large grow">{{ card.n }}</span>
          {% if card.b %}
            {% comment %}
                      Capped, like the full layout. Past a few the bullets
                      stop telling a rider anything the station name has not,
                      and a twelve-route complex wraps them onto a second line
                      — which here pushed them straight through the heading of
                      the card beside it. Three rather than four: these
                      layouts give a heading roughly half the width.
                    {% endcomment %}
                    <div class="bullet-strip{% if card.y != "subway" %} bullet-strip--tag{% endif %}">
                      {% for route in card.b %}
              {% comment %}
                The same rule the departure rows use: a bullet for one or two
                characters, a pill for a named route, and the feed's X suffix
                stripped because the diamond carries it.
              {% endcomment %}
              {% assign is_express = false %}
              {% assign glyph = route %}
              {% if route.size > 1 and route contains "X" %}
                {% assign stripped = route | remove_last: "X" %}
                {% if stripped.size == 1 %}
                  {% assign is_express = true %}
                  {% assign glyph = stripped %}
                {% endif %}
              {% endif %}
              {% if card.y == "subway" and glyph.size <= 2 %}
                <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--filled bullet{% if glyph.size == 2 %} bullet--pair{% endif %}{% if is_express %} bullet--express{% endif %}"><span>{{ glyph }}</span></span>
              {% else %}
                <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--filled type-tag">{{ glyph }}</span>
              {% endif %}
            {% endfor %}
                    </div>
                    
          {% endif %}
        </div>
        <div class="border--h-40 w--full"></div>

        {% if card.e %}
          <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--outline">Unavailable</span>
        {% else %}
          {% assign total = 0 %}
          {% for col in card.k %}{% assign total = total | plus: col.r.size %}{% endfor %}
          {% if total == 0 %}
            <div class="row-empty title title--small text--center">No upcoming departures</div>
          {% else %}
            <div class="flex flex--row gap--small w--full flex--top">
              {% for col in card.k %}
                <div class="grow basis--0 content w--full"
                     data-content-limiter="true"
                     data-content-max-height="{{ stack_height }}">
                    {% comment %}
                      The direction heading — the MTA's own platform wording
                      ("Manhattan", "Queens", "Uptown"), not north/south.

                      These layouts put both directions in one `.content`
                      rather than full.liquid's column each, so without it
                      the two merge into one undifferentiated list and a
                      rider cannot tell which way a train is going. One line
                      per direction is cheap next to that.

                      Omitted when the backend sends none, which is every
                      single-direction card: a railroad, a ferry, or a
                      subway terminal where only one direction is boardable.
                    {% endcomment %}
                    {% if col.h != "" %}
                      <div class="flex flex--row w--full">
                        <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--underline">{{ col.h }}</span>
                      </div>
                    {% endif %}
              {% for r in col.r %}
                      {% assign is_express = false %}
                      {% assign glyph = r[0] %}
                      {% if r[0].size > 1 and r[0] contains "X" %}
                        {% assign stripped = r[0] | remove_last: "X" %}
                        {% if stripped.size == 1 %}
                          {% assign is_express = true %}
                          {% assign glyph = stripped %}
                        {% endif %}
                      {% endif %}
                      {% if card.y == "subway" and glyph.size <= 2 %}
                        {% assign bullet_class = "bullet" %}
                        {% if glyph.size == 2 %}{% assign bullet_class = "bullet bullet--pair" %}{% endif %}
                        {% if is_express %}{% assign bullet_class = "bullet bullet--express" %}{% endif %}
                      {% else %}
                        {% assign bullet_class = "" %}
                      {% endif %}
                {% if r[1] == r[0] %}{% assign destination = "" %}{% else %}{% assign destination = r[1] %}{% endif %}
                {% comment %}
                  "in" toward the city, "out" away from it, nothing when the
                  direction is unknown. Only the railroads report one. The word
                  picks the triangle class; the shape is drawn in CSS.
                {% endcomment %}
                {% assign arrow = "" %}
                {% if r[4] == 1 %}
                  {% assign arrow = "in" %}
                {% elsif r[4] == -1 %}
                  {% assign arrow = "out" %}
                {% endif %}
                <div class="row-line{% if r[3] == 1 %} row-line--urgent invert rounded--small{% endif %} flex flex--row flex--between flex--center-y gap--small w--full">
                  <span class="label label--small md:label--base label--filled {{ bullet_class }}{% if bullet_class == "" %} row-badge{% endif %}">{% if bullet_class != "" %}<span>{{ glyph }}</span>{% else %}{{ r[0] }}{% endif %}</span>
                  <span class="row-dest title title--small grow">{% if arrow != "" %}<span class="row-arrow row-arrow--{{ arrow }}"></span>{% endif %}{{ destination }}</span>
                  <span class="row-time value value--xsmall">{{ r[2] }}</span>
                </div>
              {% endfor %}
                </div>
              {% endfor %}
            </div>
          {% endif %}
        {% endif %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}
      {% endfor %}
  </div>
  </div>
{% endif %}
  <div class="title_bar">
    {% comment %}
        The data's age, in New York time. A panel refreshes on an interval
        and an e-ink screen holds its last image through a failure, so
        without this there is no way to tell a quiet board from a frozen
        one. Formatted by the Worker, because Liquid cannot convert a
        timezone and the panel runs wherever the user is.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% comment %}
        The footer is always exactly two items: a label on the left and the
        timestamp on the right. A third has nowhere to go — the stamp takes
        the right edge, so anything between them is squeezed, and at a
        larger ui-scale all three collided into one unreadable line.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% comment %}
        Two words, not four bullets and not the networks spelled out.
        "NYC Subway, Bus, Rail, Ferry" was 28 characters of a bar that
        also holds a timestamp, and collided with it at a larger
        ui-scale; the bullets that replaced it read as a fifth row of
        badges rather than as a caption. The name of the board is what
        the bar is for, and `.footer-label` already clips it rather
        than pushing the stamp off the panel.
      {% endcomment %}
      <span class="title footer-label">NYC Transit</span>
      {% if a %}
        <span class="title footer-stamp">Last updated: {{ a }}</span>
      {% endif %}
  </div>

